{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>And you shall make its lamps.</b> The verb of making is written for the lamps, to teach you that they were vessels on their own, and they would move and sway from the branches. A proof for this: their calyxes and their branches were ממנה (of it), i.e. of the menorah itself; yet it is written ״ויעש את נרתיה שבעה״ — and he made its seven lamps [separately]." "<b>Its lamps, seven.</b> Corresponding to the seven days of the week, and corresponding to the seven planets — Sun, Venus, Mercury, Moon, Saturn, Jupiter, Mars — that illuminate the world." "<b>And he shall set up.</b> The priest <b>its lamps</b> — the seven — upon the seven branches. <b>And he shall illuminate</b> — the lamps, upon עבר פניה (the front side): he kindles the lamps from that side where all the branches are seen; that is what is meant by ״על עבר פני המנורה.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The word ונעים ונדים (moving and swaying) is parsed here as describing the lamps as detachable and mobile relative to the branches."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:33.698462868Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}